WEBVTT

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:03.128
Inertial dampers have failed.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.761
I can't get any response
from the field coils.

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:05.896
Did you verify
the control inputs?

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:06.928
Stand by.

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:08.908
The inputs are blocked.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.603
We've got to flush
the plasma injectors.

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:15.776
Or maybe it's just a phase
synchronization adjustment.

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:19.977
- Plasma injectors.
- Plasma injectors.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.715
Right.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.986
Field coils responding.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.423
Inertial dampers back on line.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.691
That was not fair!

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:32.959
As your flight instructor,

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.627
it's my duty to prepare you
for anything, fair or not.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.637
Clogging someone's plasma
injectors is a dirty trick.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:41.504
Yes, well, I'm famous
for my dirty tricks, you know?

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:46.945
W-What was that?

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.040
I never said the simulation
was finished.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:58.816
Computer, evasive maneuvers.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.531
I'll be ready
for anything next time.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.626
Oh, sounds like
a challenge to me.

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:19.503
Come in.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.905
I hope you have good news,
Commander.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.076
Based on this inventory,
our food reserves

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.279
look to be down
to 30 percent of capacity.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.548
Good news and bad.

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.749
We have located
an M-Class planet

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.588
and it shows high amino acid
and protein readings.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.116
That should indicate
a healthy plant life.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.150
What's bad about it?

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:37.390
It's a full day out of our way.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.692
I don't think
we have any choice.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.925
It's the first chance we've had

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.093
to replenish
our supplies in weeks.

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.102
And our long-range sensors

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:44.603
can't confirm any plant life
on the surface.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.161
Why not?

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.606
It's one strange planet,
Captain.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.378
It's shrouded.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.677
Vapors cover it like a curtain.

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.910
EM disturbances
all over the atmosphere.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.009
Some of these readings

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:01.213
suggest a planet
early in its evolution...

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:02.949
almost like a primeval Earth.

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:04.489
The people
in stellar cartography

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.287
have already nicknamed it
"Planet Hell."

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.385
So it might be
a wild goose chase.

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.123
Captain, I don't know
what we'll find

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.462
when we get to the surface...
flora or fauna, friend or foe.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.797
If we knew we'd find

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.825
another M-Class planet soon,
I'd...

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.908
Set a course for Planet Hell,
Commander.

00:04:34.473 --> 00:04:35.701
That was you?

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:36.969
That was me.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:39.442
I'm impressed.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:43.710
I keep hearing my mother say,
"Practice, Harry, practice."

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:46.718
So I ate Neelix's food
for a week

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:48.515
and used my replicator rations.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.545
Play something for me.

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:53.615
Well, I've only had it for
a few days, and I don't think...

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.286
Oh, come on!
Play something.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:56.692
Just pretend I'm not here.

00:05:06.738 --> 00:05:08.399
Play something different.

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:10.534
Different?

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:13.943
Something... not so cheerful.

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:15.139
Why?

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.670
What, you don't take requests?

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:19.081
Something bothering you, Paris?

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:23.450
Oh, Harry.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.956
I'm in trouble.

00:05:25.023 --> 00:05:26.650
What's new?

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:28.159
I think I'm in love.

00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:29.217
What's new?

00:05:29.261 --> 00:05:30.228
With Kes.

00:05:30.262 --> 00:05:31.229
Kes?!

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:32.389
I know, I know!

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:33.728
I never even saw it coming.

00:05:33.765 --> 00:05:35.858
I thought we were
just good buddies.

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:38.597
But there she was, in my arms.

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:39.661
In your arms?

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:41.639
Paris... you didn't.

00:05:41.673 --> 00:05:44.699
What? Oh! No.

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:46.438
I didn't.

00:05:46.478 --> 00:05:48.503
I wouldn't!

00:05:48.547 --> 00:05:50.947
She stumbled
during a flight simulation.

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:53.952
I caught her.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:56.784
I held her for an instant.

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:00.749
Are you going to play or not?

00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:05.491
A whole crew full of women
and I have to fall

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.328
for the one I can't have.

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:09.266
Why do I do this to myself?

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:14.097
If you ask me,
I say you enjoy it.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:15.436
Enjoy it?

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.271
Only thing that makes sense.

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:18.936
You've been doing
the same thing to yourself

00:06:19.044 --> 00:06:20.409
your whole life...

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:24.142
setting yourself up
for rejection.

00:06:24.182 --> 00:06:26.707
You must enjoy playing the part.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:31.646
Hey, don't knock it
if you haven't tried it.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.682
I just have to be more careful.

00:06:34.726 --> 00:06:36.751
I can't let myself
be alone with her.

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:39.320
There's an old
Chinese expression...

00:06:39.364 --> 00:06:42.128
"Stay out of harm's way."

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:45.834
That's not a Chinese expression.

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:47.964
If it works, use it.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:51.175
Of course, it would
help a little

00:06:51.209 --> 00:06:53.734
if Kes and I weren't going
to be stuck on the same ship

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:55.144
for the rest of our lives.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.943
That's a pessimistic view.

00:06:56.982 --> 00:06:58.142
I'm in a pessimistic mood.

00:06:58.183 --> 00:07:00.617
Play something pessimistic.

00:07:00.652 --> 00:07:03.644
Practice, Harry, practice.

00:07:19.771 --> 00:07:21.762
And then suddenly, we fell
into a subspace anomaly.

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:23.138
None of the readings
made any sense.

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:24.665
I didn't know what to do.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.507
It must have been
terrible for you.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:28.638
Uh, pepper, sweeting?

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.774
Oh. Anyway, it was quite
a learning experience.

00:07:33.318 --> 00:07:37.516
I'm sure Lieutenant Paris
is a fine teacher.

00:07:40.325 --> 00:07:43.692
Oh, I almost forgot,
the Palliantyne peas.

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:49.923
Uh...

00:07:49.968 --> 00:07:52.766
Hmm. Ever so lightly spiced.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:15.683
Analysis.

00:08:15.727 --> 00:08:18.218
High levels of trigemic vapors,

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:20.322
but it should still
support humanoid life.

00:08:20.365 --> 00:08:22.526
Sick Bay to Bridge.

00:08:22.567 --> 00:08:25.297
Please turn to your emergency
medical holographic channel.

00:08:27.472 --> 00:08:28.803
I hope you don't mind
that I've been

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.603
eavesdropping,
but I have something important

00:08:30.642 --> 00:08:31.609
to contribute.

00:08:31.643 --> 00:08:32.803
What is it, Doctor?

00:08:32.844 --> 00:08:34.709
Of course, it wouldn't
be necessary for me

00:08:34.746 --> 00:08:36.373
to eavesdrop, if I were included

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:38.474
in these discussions as a matter
of operating protocol.

00:08:38.517 --> 00:08:39.643
Doctor...

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:41.118
You should be aware
that trigemic vapors

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:42.120
at the recorded levels

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.952
can cause severe humanoid
skin irritations.

00:08:45.023 --> 00:08:47.287
Could you provide some sort of
skin protection that might help?

00:08:47.325 --> 00:08:48.519
I am whipping up

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.960
a batch of dermal osmotic
sealant as we speak.

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:52.964
Nevertheless, skin exposure

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:54.965
to the atmosphere
should be limited.

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:57.058
Noted, Doctor.
Thank you.

00:08:57.102 --> 00:09:02.506
Tell me, just how often
do you... eavesdrop?

00:09:03.875 --> 00:09:07.436
I am simply
trying to monitor issues

00:09:07.479 --> 00:09:09.674
involving the health
of the crew, Captain.

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:12.878
When activated,
the emergency medical program

00:09:12.918 --> 00:09:14.283
establishes com-links

00:09:14.319 --> 00:09:17.413
with all key areas
of the ship, and I have...

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:18.616
on occasion...

00:09:20.592 --> 00:09:23.925
I am a doctor, not a voyeur.

00:09:23.995 --> 00:09:26.225
I am programmed to be discreet.

00:09:26.264 --> 00:09:27.788
I'm sure.

00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:30.700
Commander Chakotay will discuss
operating protocols with you

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:32.761
at the earliest opportunity.

00:09:32.804 --> 00:09:35.932
In the meantime,
no more eavesdropping, Doctor.

00:09:35.974 --> 00:09:37.703
- But, Captain, I...
- That's an order.

00:09:40.345 --> 00:09:41.937
There's another problem,
Captain.

00:09:42.047 --> 00:09:44.379
We won't be able
to transport to the surface

00:09:44.416 --> 00:09:46.884
because of the EM disturbances
in the atmosphere.

00:09:46.918 --> 00:09:49.386
They'll interfere
with our com-links, too.

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:51.013
I'll have Mr. Kim take a look.

00:09:53.358 --> 00:09:56.350
Listen, why don't we take in
a holodeck program later?

00:09:56.394 --> 00:09:58.521
A Molière comedy...
that'll cheer you up.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.093
I think I'll just eat later.

00:10:08.573 --> 00:10:09.540
Damn.

00:10:09.574 --> 00:10:11.064
What do you want to do?

00:10:11.109 --> 00:10:13.134
Very simple.
We just won't sit with her.

00:10:13.178 --> 00:10:14.202
But that's rude.

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:15.736
No, that's smart.

00:10:15.781 --> 00:10:17.078
We'll make it look like

00:10:17.115 --> 00:10:19.242
we have Bridge operations
to discuss.

00:10:19.284 --> 00:10:24.221
Gentlemen, may I recommend
the Alfarian hair pasta today?

00:10:24.256 --> 00:10:25.723
Hair pasta?

00:10:25.757 --> 00:10:27.452
That is just an expression,
isn't it?

00:10:29.394 --> 00:10:30.418
Not at all.

00:10:30.462 --> 00:10:32.430
It's actually made
from the follicles

00:10:32.464 --> 00:10:34.125
of the mature Alfarian.

00:10:34.166 --> 00:10:36.930
We harvest the hair
during shedding season

00:10:36.968 --> 00:10:38.526
in early fall.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.300
It's very high in protein.

00:10:41.339 --> 00:10:42.772
Whatever.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.469
We just have a lot

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:47.478
of Bridge operations
business to discuss.

00:10:49.114 --> 00:10:50.103
Yeah.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:55.813
Mr. Kim, please report
to the Bridge.

00:10:57.522 --> 00:10:59.285
Have a nice lunch.

00:11:28.887 --> 00:11:31.355
You subclass genus.

00:11:31.389 --> 00:11:33.516
I beg your pardon.

00:11:33.558 --> 00:11:35.389
Sitting with your backs
to each other

00:11:35.427 --> 00:11:38.396
like two strangers
waiting for a transport.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.864
That's supposed to fool me?

00:11:40.899 --> 00:11:43.595
Oh. A guy just can't win.

00:11:43.635 --> 00:11:46.126
Look, there is nothing
to fool you about.

00:11:46.171 --> 00:11:47.638
Really. I swear.

00:11:47.672 --> 00:11:49.572
Liar.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.235
What the hell are you doing?!

00:11:59.417 --> 00:12:00.907
I'll kill you!

00:12:00.952 --> 00:12:02.044
It's too late!

00:12:02.087 --> 00:12:06.683
I'm betting your hair pasta
already did the trick!

00:12:06.725 --> 00:12:09.751
Janeway to Lieutenant Paris
and Mr. Neelix.

00:12:09.794 --> 00:12:11.762
Go ahead.

00:12:11.796 --> 00:12:12.763
Yes, Captain?

00:12:12.797 --> 00:12:14.230
Please report to my ready room
right away.

00:12:14.266 --> 00:12:16.097
I have something important
to discuss with you.

00:12:16.134 --> 00:12:19.194
Uh, Captain, did you say
"right away"?

00:12:19.237 --> 00:12:20.397
If we could have...

00:12:20.438 --> 00:12:21.632
That's what I said, Mr. Paris.

00:12:21.673 --> 00:12:23.004
Janeway out.

00:12:37.289 --> 00:12:39.951
Would anyone care to explain?

00:12:42.594 --> 00:12:46.030
It might be too long to go
into right now, Captain.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.733
Very well.

00:12:49.768 --> 00:12:53.329
I have a mission
for the two of you.

00:12:53.371 --> 00:12:54.395
We have reason to believe

00:12:54.439 --> 00:12:56.236
that there are food sources
on this planet,

00:12:56.274 --> 00:12:58.640
but we can't transport
to the surface.

00:12:58.677 --> 00:13:00.372
You're going to take
a shuttle down

00:13:00.412 --> 00:13:01.674
and do a little scouting.

00:13:01.713 --> 00:13:04.181
M-M-Me... and him?

00:13:04.215 --> 00:13:06.376
He's our best pilot

00:13:06.418 --> 00:13:07.442
and you're our expert

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:09.680
on edible plant species
in this Quadrant.

00:13:09.721 --> 00:13:13.122
Uh, Captain, I'm not one
to pass up a mission,

00:13:13.158 --> 00:13:14.716
but in this circumstance,

00:13:14.759 --> 00:13:17.887
maybe Ensign Baytart,
who is a fine pilot...

00:13:17.929 --> 00:13:20.625
There are electromagnetic
anomalies in the atmosphere

00:13:20.665 --> 00:13:22.394
and I would rather have you
at the helm.

00:13:22.434 --> 00:13:25.369
Am I...

00:13:25.403 --> 00:13:28.634
discerning a personal problem
here, gentlemen?

00:13:31.676 --> 00:13:33.234
Frankly, yes, Captain.

00:13:37.015 --> 00:13:38.448
Solve it.

00:13:38.483 --> 00:13:41.043
You leave at 1400 hours.

00:13:55.832 --> 00:13:57.459
We're modifying the transporter

00:13:57.501 --> 00:13:59.128
to try and penetrate
the atmosphere.

00:13:59.169 --> 00:14:01.034
So far no luck.

00:14:01.071 --> 00:14:02.698
However, we have been running

00:14:02.739 --> 00:14:04.172
computer simulations
to determine

00:14:04.207 --> 00:14:06.937
if the EM disturbances
follow any specific pattern.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:08.739
Our data analysis suggests that

00:14:08.779 --> 00:14:11.043
there may be occasional
atmospheric windows

00:14:11.081 --> 00:14:12.514
that would allow us
to get through.

00:14:12.549 --> 00:14:14.449
How occasional
and how long will they last?

00:14:14.484 --> 00:14:16.816
We should see one
every 30 hours or so,

00:14:16.853 --> 00:14:19.651
but they may only last
for a matter of minutes.

00:14:19.689 --> 00:14:20.917
I can't be sure yet.

00:14:20.957 --> 00:14:23.221
At least long enough for us
to beam up food supplies.

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:24.955
Mr. Paris, you
and Mr. Neelix will collect

00:14:25.028 --> 00:14:26.791
as many vegetables
and fruits as you can

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.424
while we wait for this
atmospheric window to open.

00:14:29.466 --> 00:14:32.594
Mr. Kim, be sure your computer
simulations include...

00:14:45.148 --> 00:14:46.581
What happened?

00:14:46.616 --> 00:14:47.583
I heard that you...

00:14:47.617 --> 00:14:48.584
Nothing happened.

00:14:48.618 --> 00:14:51.382
They said that you
and Tom Paris...

00:14:51.421 --> 00:14:55.084
Kes, we'll talk when I get back.

00:14:55.125 --> 00:14:56.752
I want to know.

00:14:56.793 --> 00:14:59.159
Neelix, is it true
that you were fighting?

00:14:59.196 --> 00:15:01.596
When I get back.

00:15:01.631 --> 00:15:02.598
But...

00:15:02.632 --> 00:15:03.621
Shuttlebay.

00:15:08.205 --> 00:15:09.672
Good afternoon.

00:15:09.706 --> 00:15:12.470
When you have a chance,
could you run an analysis

00:15:12.509 --> 00:15:14.636
of the cytokinetic results
for...

00:15:14.678 --> 00:15:15.804
I did it during lunch.

00:15:21.751 --> 00:15:22.740
Are you all right?

00:15:22.786 --> 00:15:23.878
I'm fine.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:24.909
Are you sure?

00:15:24.955 --> 00:15:26.820
- You seem a little...
- I'm fine.

00:15:27.891 --> 00:15:30.086
You know, if you skipped
lunch, the result

00:15:30.127 --> 00:15:31.856
might be a lower blood
sugar level, which could...

00:15:32.863 --> 00:15:34.888
I'm sorry.
It's in my program.

00:15:34.931 --> 00:15:37.798
I see something wrong,
I must attempt to diagnose it.

00:15:37.834 --> 00:15:39.426
There are some things
you can't cure.

00:15:43.507 --> 00:15:49.878
Neelix and Tom Paris had
a physical... fight.

00:15:49.913 --> 00:15:51.608
Over me.

00:15:51.648 --> 00:15:53.138
How delightful.

00:15:54.151 --> 00:15:55.709
Delightful?!

00:15:55.752 --> 00:15:58.220
You should consider it
a high compliment.

00:15:58.255 --> 00:16:01.850
Throughout history, men have
fought over the love of a woman.

00:16:01.892 --> 00:16:04.087
Why, I can quote you
autopsy reports

00:16:04.127 --> 00:16:06.118
from duels as far back as 1538.

00:16:06.163 --> 00:16:07.357
That's not funny.

00:16:07.397 --> 00:16:09.058
It's not meant to be.

00:16:09.099 --> 00:16:11.567
You've always been interested
in autopsies.

00:16:11.601 --> 00:16:13.831
Are all Talaxians so jealous?

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:14.928
I wouldn't know.

00:16:14.971 --> 00:16:16.438
I've only met one.

00:16:16.473 --> 00:16:17.701
I love Neelix.

00:16:17.741 --> 00:16:19.436
Why can't he just accept that?

00:16:19.476 --> 00:16:22.843
His suspicions about Tom
are completely unjustified.

00:16:22.879 --> 00:16:24.312
Are they?

00:16:24.347 --> 00:16:27.214
Lieutenant Paris' affection
for you is obvious.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:28.547
What do you mean?

00:16:28.585 --> 00:16:31.918
I mean he... desires you.

00:16:31.955 --> 00:16:33.445
That's not true.

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.082
Tom and I enjoy
each other's company.

00:16:35.125 --> 00:16:36.114
We're friends.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.057
Whenever you walk into the room,

00:16:39.095 --> 00:16:41.461
his respiration increases,
his pupils dilate

00:16:41.498 --> 00:16:44.365
and the coloration of his ears
turns decidedly orange.

00:16:44.401 --> 00:16:45.663
Until I noticed the pattern,

00:16:45.702 --> 00:16:47.294
I thought he was suffering
from Tanzian flu.

00:16:47.337 --> 00:16:49.737
It's there for anyone to see.

00:16:49.773 --> 00:16:51.968
Maybe not in the same diagnostic
detail as I see it,

00:16:52.075 --> 00:16:52.973
yet...

00:16:53.076 --> 00:16:54.976
Are you saying
that I didn't want to see it?

00:16:59.583 --> 00:17:01.915
You're only two years old.

00:17:01.985 --> 00:17:05.079
There may be a few things...

00:17:05.121 --> 00:17:08.318
you don't know
to look for in a man.

00:17:08.358 --> 00:17:10.724
In time, you'll understand.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:13.388
On my homeworld,
it's so much simpler.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:14.624
You choose a mate for life.

00:17:14.664 --> 00:17:19.158
There is no distrust, no
jealously, no envy, no betrayal.

00:17:19.202 --> 00:17:23.070
Hmm, your world must have
very dry literature.

00:17:24.541 --> 00:17:27.135
I don't know how to handle
something like this.

00:17:27.177 --> 00:17:29.975
What am I going to do?

00:17:32.515 --> 00:17:33.504
You've done nothing

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:35.541
to precipitate
this state of affairs.

00:17:35.585 --> 00:17:39.282
You may not be able to do
much to resolve it either.

00:17:39.322 --> 00:17:43.224
There are some things
you can't cure.

00:17:55.572 --> 00:17:59.906
Entry sequence at two minutes.
Mark.

00:17:59.943 --> 00:18:01.672
All systems normal.

00:18:01.711 --> 00:18:06.011
Trigemic density
at.041 and rising.

00:18:06.049 --> 00:18:10.884
We lost communication
with Voyager 30 seconds ago.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.447
You don't have to impress
me with your technobabble.

00:18:14.491 --> 00:18:17.324
I'm not trying
to impress anyone.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.760
I'm entering it
into the flight record.

00:18:21.197 --> 00:18:23.028
Look, can I make a suggestion?

00:18:23.066 --> 00:18:25.227
You're the commander
of this mission.

00:18:25.268 --> 00:18:27.099
You can make
any suggestion you want.

00:18:27.137 --> 00:18:29.401
And if you make it an order,
I'll even have to obey it.

00:18:30.607 --> 00:18:33.599
My suggestion is that we put
our personal problems aside

00:18:33.643 --> 00:18:36.339
until this mission is over,
okay?

00:18:36.379 --> 00:18:38.370
I left my personal problems
back on the ship.

00:18:38.415 --> 00:18:39.905
I can't speak for you.

00:18:39.949 --> 00:18:41.143
Fine. Let's get the job done.

00:18:41.184 --> 00:18:43.880
You get us down in one piece
and I'll take it from there.

00:18:49.192 --> 00:18:52.559
Entry sequence
at three minutes. Mark.

00:18:52.595 --> 00:18:54.028
All systems...

00:18:55.832 --> 00:18:56.856
Stand by.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:18:59.926
Have you been briefed
on shuttle operations?

00:18:59.969 --> 00:19:02.233
Ensign Baytart took me
through the basics.

00:19:02.272 --> 00:19:03.899
Of course, I, I didn't have

00:19:04.007 --> 00:19:06.475
the benefit
of your personal tutelage.

00:19:06.509 --> 00:19:07.771
Check your console.

00:19:07.811 --> 00:19:09.506
I'm reading
a power-output decline

00:19:09.546 --> 00:19:12.379
of... 15 percent.

00:19:12.415 --> 00:19:14.610
Confirmed.

00:19:14.651 --> 00:19:17.745
Warning. Vectored exhaust
controls malfunctioning.

00:19:18.888 --> 00:19:20.355
What does that mean?

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.119
We're getting
some kind of reaction

00:19:22.158 --> 00:19:23.523
in our driver coil assembly

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:25.619
from the EM anomalies
in the atmosphere.

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:30.121
I'm going to abort the mission.
Hold on.

00:19:35.171 --> 00:19:36.934
We've lost our impulse drive!

00:19:36.973 --> 00:19:38.941
Shut off the deuterium flow.

00:19:39.008 --> 00:19:40.908
I'm dumping excess fuel.

00:19:40.944 --> 00:19:42.434
Deuterium flow sealed.

00:19:42.479 --> 00:19:45.107
Setting DCA pulse order
to neutral.

00:19:45.148 --> 00:19:47.480
Engaging thrusters.

00:19:47.517 --> 00:19:49.178
Can you regain control?

00:19:49.219 --> 00:19:50.413
I'm trying, Neelix.

00:19:50.453 --> 00:19:52.478
Prepare
for an emergency landing.

00:19:52.522 --> 00:19:54.513
Send out an EMS pulse.

00:19:54.557 --> 00:19:57.048
There's still a chance
Voyager will pick it up.

00:20:00.764 --> 00:20:01.788
Captain, we're picking up

00:20:01.831 --> 00:20:03.264
a locator signal
from the shuttle.

00:20:03.299 --> 00:20:04.527
Can you track it?

00:20:04.567 --> 00:20:07.593
Their last velocity vector
indicates a steep descent.

00:20:07.637 --> 00:20:10.128
We've lost contact.

00:20:10.173 --> 00:20:11.140
Bridge to Torres.

00:20:11.174 --> 00:20:12.232
Torres here.

00:20:12.275 --> 00:20:13.799
B'Elanna,
our shuttle's in trouble.

00:20:13.843 --> 00:20:15.640
Any progress
on modifying the transporter?

00:20:17.313 --> 00:20:18.405
Negative.

00:20:18.448 --> 00:20:20.439
It's an extremely complex
interference pattern

00:20:20.483 --> 00:20:22.110
and it keeps changing.

00:20:22.152 --> 00:20:23.642
Bring in as much
help as you need.

00:20:23.686 --> 00:20:25.415
This is now a search
and rescue operation.

00:20:25.455 --> 00:20:27.116
Acknowledged.
Torres out.

00:20:27.157 --> 00:20:28.124
Keep me informed.

00:20:28.158 --> 00:20:29.147
I'll be in my ready room.

00:20:39.502 --> 00:20:41.800
I said...

00:20:41.838 --> 00:20:43.396
in one piece.

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:44.805
Sorry.

00:20:44.841 --> 00:20:46.809
I did the best I could.

00:20:46.843 --> 00:20:48.743
Any broken bones?

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:52.177
Everything seems to be working.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:54.812
I'm itching.

00:20:54.851 --> 00:20:56.546
I feel it, too.

00:20:56.586 --> 00:21:00.113
We've got atmospheric
leakage in the hull.

00:21:00.156 --> 00:21:03.216
The trigemic vapors
really sting.

00:21:03.259 --> 00:21:04.248
Here.

00:21:06.196 --> 00:21:07.925
Doc said
this would help a little.

00:21:09.132 --> 00:21:12.795
If we have atmospheric leakage,
how can we fly out of here?

00:21:12.836 --> 00:21:15.066
We can't. We'll have to wait
for Voyager to find us.

00:21:15.104 --> 00:21:16.833
We're not even sure
they're looking for us yet.

00:21:16.873 --> 00:21:18.966
We don't know
if the EMS pulse got through.

00:21:19.008 --> 00:21:20.703
B'Elanna's working
on the transporter

00:21:20.743 --> 00:21:23.837
and one of those atmospheric
windows might open up.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:24.938
In the meantime, we're going

00:21:24.981 --> 00:21:26.505
to have to look
for some kind of cover.

00:21:26.549 --> 00:21:28.642
I say we stay right here.

00:21:28.685 --> 00:21:30.550
The trigemic vapors
are going to...

00:21:30.587 --> 00:21:32.418
You're the one who wants
to go out into those vapors!

00:21:32.455 --> 00:21:34.855
Staying here is our best chance
of being found.

00:21:34.891 --> 00:21:36.188
You heard what they said.

00:21:36.226 --> 00:21:38.524
Those windows may only be open
for a matter of minutes.

00:21:38.561 --> 00:21:40.256
And we may be waiting here
for days.

00:21:40.296 --> 00:21:42.093
Exposure to these vapors
is too dangerous.

00:21:42.131 --> 00:21:43.496
We're leaving.
Grab your gear.

00:21:43.533 --> 00:21:45.694
Is that an order
or a suggestion?

00:21:45.735 --> 00:21:46.667
An order!

00:21:47.704 --> 00:21:49.035
Yes, sir.

00:21:51.474 --> 00:21:53.635
I hope you're satisfied.

00:21:53.676 --> 00:21:55.667
We were a lot more comfortable
inside the shuttle.

00:21:55.712 --> 00:21:58.010
I have red welts
all over my hands now.

00:21:58.114 --> 00:21:59.479
You know, it might be
more constructive

00:21:59.516 --> 00:22:01.450
if you could find us
something safe to eat.

00:22:01.484 --> 00:22:02.951
I certainly don't know

00:22:02.986 --> 00:22:05.477
why the ship's sensors
indicated this planet

00:22:05.522 --> 00:22:07.752
might be rich in food supplies.

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:09.382
It sure doesn't look it.

00:22:09.425 --> 00:22:11.825
Our emergency rations
won't last forever.

00:22:11.861 --> 00:22:14.591
I'll find something for us.

00:22:14.631 --> 00:22:16.258
Of course, it might not meet

00:22:16.299 --> 00:22:18.290
your personal
culinary standards.

00:22:18.334 --> 00:22:19.699
Oh, don't worry.

00:22:19.736 --> 00:22:21.203
I've learned
to lower my standards

00:22:21.237 --> 00:22:22.602
since you became cook.

00:22:24.507 --> 00:22:26.975
I'm picking up some caves
about a kilometer west of here.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:29.240
What good fortune.

00:22:33.149 --> 00:22:34.741
Come in.

00:22:34.784 --> 00:22:36.547
Harry.

00:22:36.586 --> 00:22:37.951
Any word yet?

00:22:39.756 --> 00:22:42.088
We're doing everything possible.

00:22:42.125 --> 00:22:44.389
The transporter just
doesn't want to work

00:22:44.427 --> 00:22:45.485
in that atmosphere.

00:22:45.528 --> 00:22:46.722
How are you doing?

00:22:46.763 --> 00:22:49.027
Oh, I'm all right.

00:22:52.235 --> 00:22:54.135
It's just, uh...

00:22:55.371 --> 00:22:58.738
I was very angry at them
a few hours ago.

00:22:58.775 --> 00:23:00.834
You had every right to be.

00:23:00.877 --> 00:23:03.311
And now they might both be dead.

00:23:05.982 --> 00:23:09.179
And I'm sitting here...

00:23:09.218 --> 00:23:11.880
and I'm feeling guilty
for some reason.

00:23:11.921 --> 00:23:13.252
Guilty? Why?

00:23:13.289 --> 00:23:14.950
I don't know.

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.928
But I don't like it.

00:23:19.963 --> 00:23:23.194
And it's their fault

00:23:23.232 --> 00:23:25.792
and I can't even tell them
how angry I am at them

00:23:25.835 --> 00:23:28.497
and I may never be able to tell
them how angry I am at them!

00:23:28.538 --> 00:23:29.630
So it doesn't matter, does it?

00:23:29.672 --> 00:23:30.798
Look, you know

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:32.831
if there was any way
to land that shuttle,

00:23:32.875 --> 00:23:34.672
Tom Paris got it down.

00:23:36.412 --> 00:23:39.609
And no one has stronger
survival skills than Neelix.

00:23:39.649 --> 00:23:41.617
I'm sure they'll both be fine.

00:23:41.651 --> 00:23:43.448
Unless they kill each other.

00:23:45.221 --> 00:23:47.621
They're professionals.

00:23:47.657 --> 00:23:48.988
They better be all right,

00:23:49.058 --> 00:23:51.856
because when I see them, I'm
personally going to tell them

00:23:51.894 --> 00:23:54.294
that I never intend to speak
to either one of them again.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.000
Chakotay to crew.
Battle stations.

00:24:09.612 --> 00:24:11.079
This is good.

00:24:11.114 --> 00:24:12.911
We can last a while in here.

00:24:25.825 --> 00:24:27.315
That should do it.

00:24:28.328 --> 00:24:29.920
Garnesite.

00:24:31.931 --> 00:24:34.991
This will give us
a little heat at least.

00:24:43.076 --> 00:24:46.011
We better conserve
our beacon cells.

00:25:08.835 --> 00:25:10.928
So...

00:25:12.405 --> 00:25:17.035
what is Starfleet protocol
for situations like this?

00:25:17.076 --> 00:25:18.805
Oh, there's a whole course

00:25:18.845 --> 00:25:22.508
at the Academy full of all sorts
of survival strategies.

00:25:22.548 --> 00:25:24.482
I hope you had a passing grade.

00:25:24.517 --> 00:25:26.178
B-minus.

00:25:26.219 --> 00:25:28.414
That's not very encouraging.

00:25:28.454 --> 00:25:31.946
My father was teaching
the class that year.

00:25:32.892 --> 00:25:35.622
And he gave you a B-minus?

00:25:35.661 --> 00:25:38.289
I guess he didn't
play favorites, huh?

00:25:42.969 --> 00:25:45.563
What about Talaxian protocols?

00:25:46.839 --> 00:25:49.205
I wrote my own book.

00:25:49.242 --> 00:25:52.769
My life before Voyager was
no bed of Felaran rose petals.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:55.575
No, more like a bed of...

00:25:55.615 --> 00:25:57.446
What?

00:25:59.752 --> 00:26:00.810
Nothing.

00:26:00.853 --> 00:26:02.844
You were going
to say "junk" perhaps?

00:26:02.889 --> 00:26:05.380
Go ahead, say it... junk.

00:26:05.425 --> 00:26:07.393
I was a dealer in junk.

00:26:07.427 --> 00:26:08.621
Perhaps not worthy

00:26:08.661 --> 00:26:10.652
of a woman like Kes
in your opinion.

00:26:10.696 --> 00:26:12.186
Neelix, I didn't say that.

00:26:12.231 --> 00:26:13.198
I never even...

00:26:17.336 --> 00:26:19.133
Tell me something.

00:26:19.172 --> 00:26:22.141
In that Starfleet
survival course,

00:26:22.175 --> 00:26:26.544
did your father teach you
to check for life signs

00:26:26.579 --> 00:26:28.604
before you seal yourself
inside a cave?

00:26:28.648 --> 00:26:31.082
I checked for life signs.

00:26:31.117 --> 00:26:32.584
There weren't any.

00:26:32.618 --> 00:26:35.212
There are now.

00:26:36.889 --> 00:26:38.117
Hail them.

00:26:38.157 --> 00:26:39.818
They do not respond, Captain.

00:26:49.435 --> 00:26:51.335
They're powering down
their weapon systems.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:53.804
The vessel has taken up
a position directly beneath us.

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:55.364
What do you make of it,
Lieutenant?

00:26:55.408 --> 00:26:57.968
They would seem to have adopted
a defensive posture, Captain.

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.945
I agree, but defending what?
The planet?

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:02.572
Or something on it.

00:27:09.388 --> 00:27:11.447
Whatever this life-form is...

00:27:11.491 --> 00:27:14.255
its vital signs
are awfully faint.

00:27:15.695 --> 00:27:19.222
In this atmosphere,
it could be dying of exposure.

00:27:19.265 --> 00:27:21.495
No, I don't think so.

00:27:21.534 --> 00:27:23.229
They're faint...

00:27:23.269 --> 00:27:24.759
but steady.

00:27:42.155 --> 00:27:45.352
I'd say it's becoming
more and more evident

00:27:45.391 --> 00:27:47.382
that we chose the wrong cave.

00:28:10.616 --> 00:28:13.585
This is where the life
signs are coming from.

00:28:15.087 --> 00:28:17.612
They're embryonic life-forms.

00:28:18.758 --> 00:28:21.727
These two are half formed.

00:28:21.761 --> 00:28:24.628
This one almost seems to be...

00:28:51.023 --> 00:28:54.186
The EM disturbance patterns
match the computer simulations.

00:28:54.227 --> 00:28:56.320
I'd say we'll get a shot
at an atmospheric window

00:28:56.362 --> 00:28:57.761
in about 53 minutes.

00:28:57.797 --> 00:28:59.594
Where does that leave us
with the transporter?

00:28:59.632 --> 00:29:02.829
Our range will still be limited,
but the transporter should work

00:29:02.868 --> 00:29:03.892
as long as we stay in an area

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:05.403
with reduced
electromagnetic activity.

00:29:05.438 --> 00:29:06.405
The problem is, we don't

00:29:06.439 --> 00:29:07.667
know where our shuttle
went down.

00:29:07.707 --> 00:29:09.675
So there's no way
to know how close they'll be

00:29:09.709 --> 00:29:10.733
to the window when it opens.

00:29:10.776 --> 00:29:12.266
Recommendations?

00:29:12.311 --> 00:29:14.711
Take the ship through the window
into the atmosphere.

00:29:14.747 --> 00:29:16.408
The closer you can get us
to the surface,

00:29:16.449 --> 00:29:18.576
the better our chances
for a successful beam-out.

00:29:18.618 --> 00:29:19.642
Get on it.

00:29:19.685 --> 00:29:21.448
Are you going to be
able to warn us

00:29:21.487 --> 00:29:23.079
before the window closes again?

00:29:23.122 --> 00:29:24.089
I think so.

00:29:24.123 --> 00:29:25.750
Mr. Kim, that's not an answer
I can live with.

00:29:25.791 --> 00:29:27.418
I'll try to get you
a better one, Captain.

00:29:27.460 --> 00:29:30.224
Mr. Tuvok, we need you to find
a way to get us by that ship.

00:29:30.263 --> 00:29:32.458
I have anticipated
your request, Captain.

00:29:32.498 --> 00:29:34.591
Am I correct in the assumption
that you will want to use

00:29:34.634 --> 00:29:35.658
minimal force?

00:29:35.701 --> 00:29:36.929
Assumption confirmed.

00:29:37.036 --> 00:29:38.731
I cannot guarantee success.

00:29:38.771 --> 00:29:40.534
My evaluation
of their weapon systems

00:29:40.573 --> 00:29:41.938
suggests a capacity
equal to our own.

00:29:41.974 --> 00:29:42.941
However...

00:29:42.975 --> 00:29:44.101
You've come up

00:29:44.143 --> 00:29:46.111
with an ingenious plan
to neutralize them.

00:29:46.145 --> 00:29:47.737
I have.

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:50.078
In my analysis
of our first encounter,

00:29:50.116 --> 00:29:51.447
I have detected
what I believe to be

00:29:51.484 --> 00:29:54.078
a vulnerability
in their shield configuration.

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:56.179
There appears to be
a slight phase retraction

00:29:56.222 --> 00:29:57.587
when auxiliary power
is transferred

00:29:57.623 --> 00:29:59.386
to their aft weapon systems.

00:29:59.425 --> 00:30:02.087
A covariant phaser pulse
into their aft control systems

00:30:02.128 --> 00:30:04.289
might disable
their entire weapons array.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:07.959
That was my ingenious plan,
Commander.

00:30:08.034 --> 00:30:09.262
You are correct.

00:30:09.302 --> 00:30:10.269
Very well.

00:30:10.303 --> 00:30:11.361
Red Alert.

00:30:11.404 --> 00:30:12.371
Mr. Kim, set coordinates.

00:30:12.405 --> 00:30:13.963
Torres, speed at 2,000 kph.

00:30:14.006 --> 00:30:14.973
Aye, Captain.

00:30:15.007 --> 00:30:16.304
Chakotay, take the helm.

00:30:16.342 --> 00:30:17.502
Coordinates entered.

00:30:17.543 --> 00:30:20.239
Engage.

00:30:22.014 --> 00:30:24.141
This creature's going
to have the ability

00:30:24.183 --> 00:30:25.150
to stand upright.

00:30:25.184 --> 00:30:27.675
It has the skeletal system
of a humanoid.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:31.178
It's cold-blooded,
has reptilian epidermis.

00:30:31.223 --> 00:30:33.248
Its brain
is significantly larger

00:30:33.292 --> 00:30:35.317
than most reptilian species.

00:30:35.361 --> 00:30:36.328
I'd say we're dealing

00:30:36.362 --> 00:30:38.125
with a sentient
life-form here, Neelix.

00:30:38.164 --> 00:30:39.995
If this species is sentient,

00:30:40.032 --> 00:30:42.159
wouldn't that suggest

00:30:42.201 --> 00:30:44.635
that its parents might
return to care for it?

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:46.604
It's hard to say.

00:30:46.639 --> 00:30:48.573
Most common reptiles
lay their eggs

00:30:48.607 --> 00:30:50.575
and never return
to protect them.

00:30:50.609 --> 00:30:51.974
I think we've established

00:30:52.011 --> 00:30:53.672
we're not dealing
with a common reptile.

00:30:53.713 --> 00:30:54.737
True.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:56.008
And if this thing does
have a mother,

00:30:56.082 --> 00:30:58.550
I'd say we don't want
to be around when she gets back.

00:30:58.584 --> 00:30:59.573
Come on.

00:30:59.618 --> 00:31:01.313
We can't just leave it here.

00:31:01.354 --> 00:31:02.446
We're not leaving it here.

00:31:02.488 --> 00:31:03.955
Its mother left it here.

00:31:04.056 --> 00:31:05.546
We just happened to wander in.

00:31:05.591 --> 00:31:07.559
But we blocked off the cave.

00:31:07.593 --> 00:31:09.288
The mother might have been
trapped outside.

00:31:09.328 --> 00:31:12.729
We'll leave the door open
for her when we go, okay?

00:31:12.765 --> 00:31:14.733
Look, it's not that simple.

00:31:16.001 --> 00:31:18.128
What if she can sense
we were here?

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:19.967
She... she might
be able to smell

00:31:20.039 --> 00:31:22.200
that faux lime
aftershave of yours.

00:31:22.241 --> 00:31:24.539
I'm sure you realize

00:31:24.577 --> 00:31:26.442
many species won't return
to a nest

00:31:26.479 --> 00:31:28.140
after it's been tampered with.

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:31.672
Well, there's not much we can
do about that now, is there?

00:31:31.717 --> 00:31:33.981
We should just leave all this
the way we found it.

00:31:34.053 --> 00:31:34.985
No.

00:31:35.087 --> 00:31:35.985
Neelix!

00:31:36.088 --> 00:31:37.988
This creature is
our responsibility.

00:31:38.023 --> 00:31:39.320
Ours?

00:31:39.358 --> 00:31:41.519
The moment we came in here,
it became our responsibility.

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:43.619
And you expect to take care
of this thing until what?

00:31:43.662 --> 00:31:46.222
It graduates
from high school, college?

00:31:46.265 --> 00:31:48.130
And what if Mom
doesn't come back?

00:31:48.167 --> 00:31:50.158
Are you planning to bring
this with us back on the ship?

00:31:50.202 --> 00:31:53.399
Look, if she does return
and accepts the hatchling,

00:31:53.439 --> 00:31:54.838
it'll solve everything.

00:31:54.874 --> 00:31:57.536
We just have to wait
and see what she does.

00:31:57.576 --> 00:31:59.271
I don't know how it is
in your Quadrant,

00:31:59.311 --> 00:32:02.041
but in mine we don't
abandon a newborn...

00:32:03.149 --> 00:32:04.548
thing.

00:32:08.154 --> 00:32:09.781
Fine.

00:32:09.822 --> 00:32:11.619
Fine, you made your point.

00:32:13.793 --> 00:32:17.229
I officially designate you
its godfather.

00:32:19.064 --> 00:32:20.691
It stopped crying.

00:32:20.733 --> 00:32:21.825
It's shivering.

00:32:21.867 --> 00:32:24.358
That's normal... isn't it?

00:32:24.403 --> 00:32:28.430
I mean, newborn life-forms
do shiver, don't they?

00:32:28.474 --> 00:32:32.410
To be honest, I haven't
been around many newborns.

00:32:33.979 --> 00:32:35.446
Me neither.

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:48.158
Oh... look at this, Paris.

00:32:49.161 --> 00:32:50.492
It stopped shivering.

00:32:59.305 --> 00:33:00.397
It's asleep.

00:33:03.843 --> 00:33:06.141
Forget what I said, Neelix.

00:33:06.178 --> 00:33:07.839
You're not its godfather.

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:09.177
You're its godmother.

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:19.385
The atmospheric window
is forming, Captain.

00:33:19.425 --> 00:33:21.052
30 degrees
off the predicted coordinates.

00:33:21.093 --> 00:33:22.958
How soon will we have
maximum aperture, Mr. Kim?

00:33:23.062 --> 00:33:24.120
Nine minutes.

00:33:24.163 --> 00:33:25.790
All right, Commander,
let's try to draw some fire

00:33:25.831 --> 00:33:26.957
from their aft weapon systems.

00:33:27.066 --> 00:33:28.397
Bring us within ten kilometers.

00:33:34.273 --> 00:33:35.638
Shields at 80 percent.

00:33:35.674 --> 00:33:38.541
Minor damage
to our port nacelle.

00:33:38.577 --> 00:33:39.976
Rerouting power
to lateral thrusters.

00:33:40.079 --> 00:33:41.239
Tuvok?

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:43.305
So far, I'm not detecting
the same vulnerability

00:33:43.349 --> 00:33:44.611
in their shields this time,
Captain.

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:46.777
We may have to resort
to additional force.

00:33:46.819 --> 00:33:48.184
Arm photons.

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:49.187
Ready.

00:33:49.221 --> 00:33:50.188
Prepare to...

00:33:50.222 --> 00:33:51.189
Stand by, Captain.

00:33:51.223 --> 00:33:53.214
I'm detecting an opening.

00:33:59.732 --> 00:34:01.927
Direct hit to their
aft control systems.

00:34:02.001 --> 00:34:03.730
Their weapons are disabled.

00:34:03.769 --> 00:34:05.828
Commander, proceed
to Mr. Kim's coordinates.

00:34:05.871 --> 00:34:07.498
One-half impulse.

00:34:10.242 --> 00:34:12.836
Oh, come on.
Come on.

00:34:12.878 --> 00:34:15.938
Doesn't seem to care
for Starfleet rations.

00:34:16.048 --> 00:34:18.380
If I were in my kitchen,

00:34:18.417 --> 00:34:22.547
I could cook you up some
delightful leola root broth.

00:34:22.588 --> 00:34:23.919
Are you serious?

00:34:23.956 --> 00:34:27.084
Ugh, I hope I never
see another leola root,

00:34:27.126 --> 00:34:28.423
and you are certainly
not feeding

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:30.894
any of that hideous
broth to my niece...

00:34:30.930 --> 00:34:33.558
or nephew...

00:34:33.599 --> 00:34:35.191
whatever it is.

00:34:38.671 --> 00:34:40.935
Its heartbeat is getting weaker.

00:34:40.973 --> 00:34:43.271
I don't like it.

00:34:43.309 --> 00:34:45.174
Come on, kid.

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:46.871
You've got to eat something.

00:34:46.912 --> 00:34:51.315
It seems far more interested
in my jacket than your food.

00:34:55.087 --> 00:34:58.420
Neelix, it's not your jacket.

00:34:58.457 --> 00:35:01.324
It's the residue of the trigemic
vapors we walked through.

00:35:01.360 --> 00:35:02.918
Think about it.

00:35:02.995 --> 00:35:04.463
Voyager's sensors

00:35:04.596 --> 00:35:06.928
picked up high levels
of proteins and amino acids,

00:35:06.965 --> 00:35:08.523
which we assumed meant
this was a planet

00:35:08.567 --> 00:35:11.161
filled with vast supplies
of edible plant life.

00:35:11.203 --> 00:35:12.568
We were certainly
wrong about that.

00:35:12.604 --> 00:35:16.404
The residue is a concentration
of proteins and amino acids.

00:35:16.441 --> 00:35:19.410
The perfect source of nutrition
for the hatchling.

00:35:19.444 --> 00:35:22.072
The source we cut off
when we blocked the cave.

00:35:22.113 --> 00:35:25.173
Come on, we've got
some rocks to move.

00:35:27.853 --> 00:35:29.844
The atmospheric window
is at full aperture, Captain.

00:35:29.888 --> 00:35:31.355
We should have
at least 17 minutes

00:35:31.389 --> 00:35:32.549
before it starts to close.

00:35:32.591 --> 00:35:34.491
Captain, the alien vessel
is in pursuit.

00:35:34.526 --> 00:35:36.460
Are their weapon systems
back on line already?

00:35:36.495 --> 00:35:38.554
Negative.
They are still disabled.

00:35:38.597 --> 00:35:39.962
They couldn't be preparing
to ram us, could they?

00:35:40.031 --> 00:35:40.963
Hold your course.

00:35:41.032 --> 00:35:42.260
We need to get
through that window,

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:43.961
but prepare to take
evasive action if necessary.

00:35:44.069 --> 00:35:45.058
Understood.

00:35:45.103 --> 00:35:47.731
Alien vessel closing
to 1,000 kilometers.

00:35:47.772 --> 00:35:49.467
Five hundred.

00:35:49.508 --> 00:35:51.601
Three hundred.

00:35:51.643 --> 00:35:52.610
It has passed us

00:35:52.644 --> 00:35:54.168
and is proceeding
toward the surface.

00:35:54.212 --> 00:35:55.645
Take us down, Commander.

00:36:07.425 --> 00:36:09.017
It's not breathing!

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:10.550
I don't think it's breathing!

00:36:10.595 --> 00:36:13.587
Come on, suck in some of those

00:36:13.632 --> 00:36:14.599
hearty vapors.

00:36:15.800 --> 00:36:17.358
It's just too weak.

00:36:20.438 --> 00:36:23.305
There must be something in here
we can use to resuscitate it.

00:36:23.341 --> 00:36:25.571
Neelix, we did
the best we could.

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:26.736
No!

00:36:26.778 --> 00:36:28.177
It's our fault, it's our fault.

00:36:28.213 --> 00:36:29.805
If we hadn't come...

00:36:29.848 --> 00:36:32.942
Here. Cordrazine.

00:36:32.984 --> 00:36:34.042
This is a stimulant.

00:36:34.085 --> 00:36:35.575
You can't pump it full of drugs

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:37.451
without knowing
its body chemistry.

00:36:37.489 --> 00:36:39.150
So we should just let it die?

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:46.864
Give me that.

00:36:48.833 --> 00:36:52.462
When I was a kid,
a baby bird fell out of a nest

00:36:52.504 --> 00:36:54.870
and landed on the windowsill
next to my bed.

00:36:54.906 --> 00:36:56.635
We had to feed it water
with an eyedropper

00:36:56.675 --> 00:36:58.336
to keep it alive.

00:36:58.376 --> 00:37:00.241
What are you doing?

00:37:00.278 --> 00:37:02.678
Filling the eyedropper.

00:37:02.714 --> 00:37:04.909
Can you hold its mouth open?

00:37:10.956 --> 00:37:12.389
Do it again.

00:37:12.424 --> 00:37:13.755
Again.

00:37:17.662 --> 00:37:18.924
It's working.

00:37:18.997 --> 00:37:20.123
It's working!

00:37:21.499 --> 00:37:23.467
Life signs are getting stronger.

00:37:23.501 --> 00:37:24.934
You did it!

00:37:25.003 --> 00:37:26.664
We did it.

00:37:26.705 --> 00:37:30.072
You know, I never would have
thought of that hypospray, if...

00:37:30.108 --> 00:37:31.905
The vapors.

00:37:31.943 --> 00:37:34.104
Who cares?

00:37:49.227 --> 00:37:51.092
Sensors are operating
as well as can be expected

00:37:51.129 --> 00:37:52.596
under these conditions.

00:37:52.631 --> 00:37:55.293
Either we're just not close
enough to where they landed

00:37:55.333 --> 00:37:57.801
to pick up their com signals,
or they never landed.

00:37:57.836 --> 00:38:00.168
Their shuttle might have
burned up in the atmosphere.

00:38:00.205 --> 00:38:01.729
I'm not willing
to concede that yet.

00:38:01.773 --> 00:38:02.740
Any sign of the alien ship?

00:38:02.774 --> 00:38:03.900
No, Captain.
We've lost them.

00:38:03.975 --> 00:38:06.273
Mr. Kim, how much longer before
that window starts to close?

00:38:06.311 --> 00:38:07.539
Nine minutes, 20 seconds.

00:38:07.579 --> 00:38:08.910
Chakotay, extend our
surveillance pattern.

00:38:09.014 --> 00:38:10.311
If we don't find them
in five minutes,

00:38:10.348 --> 00:38:11.372
we'll head back to the window.

00:38:11.416 --> 00:38:12.747
Aye, Captain.

00:38:27.999 --> 00:38:29.523
I'm sorry.

00:38:29.567 --> 00:38:30.966
Hmm?

00:38:35.173 --> 00:38:37.437
I feel I owe you an apology.

00:38:40.311 --> 00:38:42.142
No, you don't.

00:38:43.515 --> 00:38:46.814
I had no right to push
that pasta in your lap.

00:38:46.851 --> 00:38:51.083
Well... think of it this way.

00:38:51.122 --> 00:38:52.749
It saved me
from having to eat it.

00:38:52.791 --> 00:38:54.122
Nothing personal,

00:38:54.159 --> 00:38:57.219
but I just don't have an
affinity for hair in my food.

00:38:57.262 --> 00:39:00.254
I haven't been fair to you.

00:39:02.367 --> 00:39:06.463
You know, I hear people talking
about you in the Mess Hall.

00:39:07.639 --> 00:39:10.073
No kidding.

00:39:10.108 --> 00:39:12.372
What do they say?

00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:15.311
Oh, that you used to be a coward

00:39:15.346 --> 00:39:17.314
and couldn't be trusted.

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:20.679
It's typical of Kes
that she would

00:39:20.719 --> 00:39:23.449
befriend someone like you...

00:39:23.488 --> 00:39:26.548
someone who really
needs a friend.

00:39:26.591 --> 00:39:30.391
And I guess I...
just got a little jealous.

00:39:30.428 --> 00:39:33.226
I got more
than a little jealous.

00:39:34.899 --> 00:39:36.457
I realize now I was wrong.

00:39:48.813 --> 00:39:50.371
Neelix, I told you the truth

00:39:50.415 --> 00:39:53.407
when I said that nothing
happened between me and Kes.

00:39:54.986 --> 00:39:56.578
I know.

00:39:56.621 --> 00:39:59.385
And I promise you,
nothing ever will,

00:39:59.424 --> 00:40:01.324
but...

00:40:03.361 --> 00:40:05.454
you weren't wrong about me.

00:40:06.564 --> 00:40:07.553
What do you mean?

00:40:09.134 --> 00:40:10.123
I...

00:40:13.071 --> 00:40:16.905
Well, I guess the only way
to say it is to say it.

00:40:16.941 --> 00:40:19.466
I am attracted to Kes.

00:40:20.845 --> 00:40:21.812
Oh?

00:40:21.846 --> 00:40:23.871
To be honest,
if you weren't around,

00:40:23.915 --> 00:40:25.849
I'd be knocking at her door.

00:40:25.884 --> 00:40:28.284
But I respect you, Neelix.

00:40:28.319 --> 00:40:30.810
And that's that.

00:40:33.691 --> 00:40:36.387
Everything you heard
those guys saying about me...

00:40:36.427 --> 00:40:39.225
well, it was true.

00:40:39.264 --> 00:40:41.994
But it's not who I am anymore.

00:40:42.066 --> 00:40:44.500
At least not who I want to be.

00:40:44.536 --> 00:40:49.599
This upside-down mission
to the wrong side of the galaxy

00:40:49.641 --> 00:40:52.269
is giving me a second chance

00:40:52.310 --> 00:40:55.074
and I don't intend to blow it.

00:40:56.581 --> 00:40:58.549
I see.

00:40:58.583 --> 00:41:02.246
Well, in a way,
I can't say I blame you.

00:41:03.721 --> 00:41:06.087
She is very attractive.

00:41:09.494 --> 00:41:12.622
She's devoted to you, you know?

00:41:12.664 --> 00:41:15.064
Well, I did save her life.

00:41:15.099 --> 00:41:17.192
I'm sure she's grateful.

00:41:17.235 --> 00:41:19.601
Oh, she's a lot
more than grateful.

00:41:19.637 --> 00:41:22.470
She loves you, Neelix.

00:41:22.507 --> 00:41:24.168
I wouldn't stand a chance.

00:41:24.209 --> 00:41:25.437
Nobody would.

00:41:26.678 --> 00:41:27.975
There are others?

00:41:28.012 --> 00:41:32.608
If you ever doubt yourself,
just look into her eyes.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.084
See the way she looks at you.

00:41:35.119 --> 00:41:37.849
You'll never doubt
yourself again.

00:41:39.457 --> 00:41:44.190
And as far as I'm concerned,
I'll just be her friend.

00:41:44.229 --> 00:41:47.756
I mean, if you don't mind.

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.792
I don't pick Kes' friends
for her.

00:41:53.504 --> 00:41:55.369
Just my own.

00:41:59.477 --> 00:42:01.945
Voyager, this is Lieutenant
Tom Paris and Mr. Neelix.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:02.947
Do you read?

00:42:02.981 --> 00:42:03.948
We do, Mr. Paris.

00:42:03.982 --> 00:42:04.949
Are you all right?

00:42:04.983 --> 00:42:06.245
Aside from a nasty rash, yes.

00:42:06.284 --> 00:42:07.774
Have you encountered life-forms?

00:42:07.819 --> 00:42:08.945
Actually, yes.

00:42:09.053 --> 00:42:10.577
Are they hostile?

00:42:10.622 --> 00:42:12.089
No.

00:42:12.123 --> 00:42:14.455
Just... young.

00:42:14.492 --> 00:42:15.720
Young?

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:16.954
Yes, we found a nest,

00:42:17.061 --> 00:42:19.052
and one
of the creatures hatched.

00:42:19.097 --> 00:42:20.359
Be aware, we encountered

00:42:20.398 --> 00:42:22.127
a hostile ship
of unknown origin,

00:42:22.166 --> 00:42:23.565
and we're showing that ship
on the surface

00:42:23.601 --> 00:42:25.159
approximately one kilometer
from your position.

00:42:25.203 --> 00:42:27.467
We're attempting to lock on
to you for transport.

00:42:27.505 --> 00:42:28.472
Stand by.

00:42:28.506 --> 00:42:29.564
We can't leave yet.

00:42:29.607 --> 00:42:30.574
What? Why?

00:42:30.608 --> 00:42:32.633
What if the mother rejects it?

00:42:32.677 --> 00:42:34.508
Neelix, one thing's for sure...

00:42:34.545 --> 00:42:36.206
whether the mother
rejects it or not,

00:42:36.247 --> 00:42:37.714
she will reject you and me.

00:42:37.749 --> 00:42:40.274
We promised each other
we wouldn't abandon it until...

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:44.185
Away team, we're showing
a life-form moving

00:42:44.222 --> 00:42:45.189
toward your position.

00:42:45.223 --> 00:42:46.190
Prepare to transport.

00:42:46.224 --> 00:42:47.691
Captain, this is Neelix.

00:42:47.725 --> 00:42:49.989
Are you in a particular
rush to leave?

00:42:50.061 --> 00:42:51.688
Did you hear what I just said,
Mr. Neelix?

00:42:51.729 --> 00:42:53.128
Yes, but, uh...

00:42:53.164 --> 00:42:54.722
we're not quite
finished here yet.

00:42:54.766 --> 00:42:55.733
Finished with what?

00:42:55.767 --> 00:42:56.893
We have an atmospheric window

00:42:56.935 --> 00:42:58.197
closing
in less than six minutes.

00:42:58.236 --> 00:42:59.863
And another one
will be coming in how long?

00:42:59.904 --> 00:43:00.871
Days.

00:43:00.905 --> 00:43:01.872
Is there a problem?

00:43:01.906 --> 00:43:03.601
The problem is, Captain,

00:43:03.641 --> 00:43:05.302
if we leave
our little hatchling here

00:43:05.343 --> 00:43:06.503
and the mother rejects it,

00:43:06.544 --> 00:43:08.842
it might die of exposure
before we get back.

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:10.177
I appreciate your concern

00:43:10.214 --> 00:43:11.181
for its welfare...

00:43:11.215 --> 00:43:12.273
How long can you give us,
Captain?

00:43:14.218 --> 00:43:15.879
One minute, ten seconds, max.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:16.909
We'll take it!

00:43:16.955 --> 00:43:17.944
Lock on to us, Voyager.

00:43:18.022 --> 00:43:19.683
If you hear muffled screams,

00:43:19.724 --> 00:43:21.954
consider that a request
for a beam-out.

00:43:51.322 --> 00:43:53.153
There's nothing
like a family reunion.

00:43:54.659 --> 00:43:56.149
Voyager, two to beam out!

00:43:58.029 --> 00:43:59.462
As soon as you're ready.

00:44:06.104 --> 00:44:07.401
Kes!

00:44:07.438 --> 00:44:09.429
We didn't know if you
were dead or alive.

00:44:09.474 --> 00:44:10.463
What happened?

00:44:12.777 --> 00:44:14.244
I became an uncle.

00:44:14.278 --> 00:44:16.075
And I'm a godmother.

00:44:18.883 --> 00:44:21.943
I'd say this is
the special occasion

00:44:21.986 --> 00:44:24.648
I've been waiting for
to open up that bottle

00:44:24.689 --> 00:44:27.419
of Potak cold fowl
I've been saving.

00:44:27.458 --> 00:44:28.823
Cold fowl in a bottle?

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:30.828
That's just an expression,
isn't it?

00:44:30.862 --> 00:44:33.262
No. It's actually made
from the glandular secretions

00:44:33.297 --> 00:44:36.425
of an adult dunghill bird
found only on Potak III.

00:44:36.467 --> 00:44:40.733
It's very rare, very exquisite.

00:44:40.772 --> 00:44:42.831
There are, I believe

00:44:42.874 --> 00:44:45.843
only 27 bottles
in existence at this time.

00:44:45.877 --> 00:44:47.811
It has a rather smoky flavor,

00:44:47.845 --> 00:44:50.678
uh... going well
with strong meaty dishes,

00:44:50.715 --> 00:44:53.275
but I prefer it for sipping...
